# Line endings for files that are EXECUTED BY A LINUX SHELL.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, so a checkout
# rewrites tracked text files to CRLF. That is harmless for TypeScript and for
# the Python scripts BookForge spawns (CPython accepts CRLF), and it is FATAL for
# a shell script that runs inside WSL: bash reads `#!/bin/bash\r` as an
# interpreter path ending in a carriage return and answers
# "/bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory", and every `if`/`fi`
# in the body inherits the same stray byte.
# `eol=lf` pins these to LF in the WORKING TREE regardless of autocrlf, which is
# what the WSL side actually reads (BookForge hands wsl.exe a /mnt/c path to the
# checked-out file — it does not stage a copy).
# The .py files beside them are pinned for a narrower reason: they are compared
# against, and were copied from, the campaign originals, and a platform-dependent
# byte difference makes "is this still the same patch" unanswerable by hashing.
# The .txt beside them is requirements-narrator-runtime.txt, which is read INSIDE
# the guest twice — `pip install -r` and the installer's sed/grep that pulls the
# `# import:` annotations out of it — so a trailing CR would ride into a pip
# requirement and into a module name the doctor then probes for.
# The .yaml beside them is higgs_default_frames7500.yaml, a vllm-omni deploy
# profile the installer copies into the env and vllm-omni reads INSIDE the guest.
# It is pinned for a stronger reason than the doctor's hash (which is CR-stripped
# on both sides, like the launcher's, so it would not notice): the training
# side's certificates bind to this file's EXACT BYTES, sha256
# 24d288f193eaa8c5…, and a CRLF checkout would deploy a file that is not the one
# any certificate was measured against. YAML parses either way, so nothing would
# fail — it would just no longer be the certified profile.
# narrator's OWN copy of the launcher and the deploy profile
# (python/narrator/engine/higgs/launch/), shipped as package data and run by
# bash inside a Linux guest exactly as the electron/scripts/higgs/ pair is. Both
# reasons above apply to it verbatim - and one more: tools/test-higgs-engine.js
# asserts the two pairs are BYTE-IDENTICAL, which cannot be true if only one of
# them is pinned to LF.
python/narrator/engine/higgs/launch/*.sh text eol=lf
python/narrator/engine/higgs/launch/*.yaml text eol=lf
electron/scripts/higgs/*.sh text eol=lf
electron/scripts/higgs/*.py text eol=lf
electron/scripts/higgs/*.txt text eol=lf
electron/scripts/higgs/*.yaml text eol=lf
python/narrator/tests/golden/** text eol=lf
electron/scripts/vllm/*.sh text eol=lf

# shared/crucible/bookforge.module.json is a VENDORED COPY of the crucible
# repo's modules/bookforge.module.json, which that repo GENERATES from its
# manifests (scripts/gen-modules.py; PHASE13-OPERATOR.md section 5.4). The whole
# claim this file makes is "byte for byte", and tools/test-crucible-module-file.js
# checks exactly that against the crucible checkout when one is present. Under
# core.autocrlf=true a checkout would rewrite our copy to CRLF while the
# generator keeps emitting LF, so the two would differ on every Windows machine
# for a reason that has nothing to do with what either file says. `-text` is
# stronger than `eol=lf` on purpose: it tells git this file's bytes are never
# translated in either direction, which is the property being asserted.
shared/crucible/bookforge.module.json -text

# THE SAME CLAIM, THE SAME PIN, FOR THE TWO FILES VENDORED FROM FOUNDRY.
# Found 2026-09-16 by the Foundry session while it re-vendored at 0.6.6, and
# MEASURED here rather than assumed: `git -c core.autocrlf=true checkout-index`
# writes foundry.module.json as 467 bytes with 32 CRLF where canonical is 435
# LF, and model-lineup.json as 2111 against 2036. Our own module file was
# already pinned above; these two were not, so a fresh clone on this machine
# produced files that no longer matched the bytes they claim to be.
#
# WHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT, which is the part worth keeping. The JSON still
# parses, every working copy is whatever that machine's git wrote, and any
# check that compares PARSED VALUES agrees. It surfaces only in something that
# hashes the FILE. A blob comparison does not catch it either: git blobs are
# LF-normalised, so the corruption exists only on the working-tree side.
foundry-app/shared/foundry.module.json -text
foundry-app/shared/model-lineup.json -text
